## The V-Prong Test

Before you finalize any princess cut setting, do this one check.

Look at the corner of the diamond head. A V-prong wraps around the corner of the stone — it looks like a small metal V sitting on the point. A round prong sits at the side of the stone — it leaves the corner completely exposed.

If you can't tell from the product photo, call Blue Nile or check the setting's spec sheet. The V-prong detail is a listed spec, not something you have to guess.

Most princess solitaire settings offer a V-prong option when ordering. Confirm before you pay.

Why this matters: the corner of a princess cut is a 90-degree crystal point. That point is structurally weaker than any part of a round diamond. A round prong transfers shock force directly to that corner.

Over time — sometimes in weeks — this causes corner chipping. The chip happens at the base of the corner and isn't covered under standard insurance without a separate jewelry floater.

> **Farzana's take:** The most common princess cut claim I see is corner chip from standard prongs. Someone comes in with a ring they bought six months ago, a chipped corner, and a setting that was clearly never designed for a princess. I always ask: did anyone tell you about V-prongs before you bought? The answer is almost always no. That's why I check the setting spec sheet before recommending anything. One phone call prevents a $400–$800 repair.

---

## The Round Halo Trap

The most common mistake I see with princess cut ring shopping: the buyer falls in love with a round halo setting photo online, buys it, and then realizes in person that the round halo makes the princess diamond look like a round.

Here's why it happens. A round halo places a ring of small round diamonds in a circular pattern around the center stone. On a round diamond, this adds brilliance and perceived size.

On a princess cut, the same halo circles the outside of a square stone. From above, the circular halo dominates the silhouette. The square geometry of the princess disappears inside it.

The princess reads as round. You spent money specifically on a princess cut for its square shape, and a round halo erases that shape completely.

The alternative is a hidden halo, a square halo, or no halo at all. The [Petite Hidden Halo](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/petite-hidden-halo-solitaire-plus-diamond-engagement-ring-in-14k-yellow-gold-item-192506?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) at $1,255 sits below the table, invisible from above. The princess square stays intact.

---

## The 5 Setting Mistakes — and What They Cost

The number 5 appears deliberately in this guide's title. Here are the 5 specific mistakes buyers make with princess cut engagement ring settings, in order of how often they happen.

### Mistake 1: Choosing a 4-Prong Round Setting for a Princess Cut

Standard solitaire prongs are round-tipped and positioned at the cardinal points of the head (north, south, east, west). For a round diamond, that's perfect. For a princess, those four positions land at the sides of the stone — leaving all four corners bare.

The fix: confirm V-prong before purchase. Cost to fix after: $400–$800 resetting fee if you need to switch settings.

### Mistake 2: Buying a Round Halo That Erases the Princess Shape

A circular halo makes the princess diamond read as round from above. You bought a princess cut for the square shape. The round halo removes it.

The fix: choose a hidden halo (sits below the table) or a square halo (follows the perimeter). Cost to fix after: $800–$1,500 setting exchange.

### Mistake 3: Forgetting to Add the Setting Price to the Budget

The most common budget mistake in ring buying: pricing the stone, falling in love with it, then discovering the setting costs an additional $1,000–$4,000. At that point, buyers either compromise on stone quality or go over budget.

The fix: price stone + setting together before you start. The tables in this guide show combined prices.

### Mistake 4: Choosing Platinum Prongs, Then Re-Tipping in Yellow Gold

When a prong breaks or wears, a jeweler re-tips it — building the prong back up with new metal. If your setting is platinum, the repair must be done in platinum.

Some local jewelers only stock yellow or white gold solder and will re-tip with the wrong metal. The mismatch is visible.

The fix: specify platinum re-tipping explicitly when bringing a platinum ring in for repair. Or choose a 14k setting and use 14k solder everywhere without confusion.

### Mistake 5: Ignoring the Corner Chip Risk Until It's Too Late

A chipped princess corner is a $400–$800 repair that insurance covers only if you have a jewelry floater with a recent appraisal. Most buyers don't discover this until the chip happens.

The fix: buy a V-prong setting from the start. If you already have a princess ring without V-prongs, get a jewelry appraisal and a separate floater policy now — before anything happens. It costs $30–$60 per year.

> **Farzana's take:** I keep a short list of the settings I trust for daily-wear princess rings. The Petite Hidden Halo at $1,255 and the French Pavé at $2,140 are at the top. Both have V-prong options, both have been through thousands of orders, and both have real review data. When I show buyers the 1,645-review Classic Four Prong Solitaire in Platinum, the first thing I tell them is: confirm V-prong when you order. Do not assume.

