## The V-Prong Buying Checklist

Before you click buy, run through this. Takes 3 minutes and saves you from the most common mistake.

**Step 1: Confirm prong type in the setting specifications.**
Open the setting page. Scroll to specifications or details. Look for "V-prong" or "V-shaped prong." If you don't see it listed, go to step 2.

**Step 2: Check the 360° ring viewer.**
Zoom into the corner of the setting head. A V-prong wraps around the corner — you'll see a pointed metal tip at the corner. A round prong sits at the side, leaving the corner exposed.

**Step 3: Contact the retailer if still unsure.**
Blue Nile has chat support. One question: "Does this setting have V-prongs for a princess cut diamond?" If the answer is no, pick a different setting from the list in this guide.

**Step 4: Confirm stone orientation.**
A princess cut can be set at 0° (corners at 12, 3, 6, 9 o'clock) or 45° (corners at the diagonals). Neither is wrong, but 0° is traditional and easier to get V-prongs on. Ask about the 45° rotation if you prefer the diamond-within-a-diamond look.

**Step 5: Price the total ring before you commit.**
Stone price + setting price = total ring price. Add both to your cart before committing to either. The tables in this guide show what real totals look like.

---

## The Right Order of Operations

Most buyers do this backwards. They fall in love with a stone, buy it, then discover they can't afford the setting they wanted.

Here's the right sequence:

**1. Set your total ring budget first.** Not a stone budget. A total budget. $4,000 total means $4,000 for stone plus setting — not $4,000 for the stone alone.

**2. Allocate roughly 70% to the stone, 30% to the setting.** A $4,000 budget = $2,800 stone, $1,200 setting. A $5,000 budget = $3,500 stone, $1,500 setting. These ratios work for most princess solitaires.

**3. Filter for the stone first.** A [GIA 1ct G-VS2 at $2,212](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28961307?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) fits a $4,000 budget with room for the [Classic Four Prong Solitaire in Platinum at $1,180](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/classic-four-prong-solitaire-engagement-ring-in-platinum-item-194156?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational). Total: $3,392.

**4. Confirm the setting accepts princess cut stones.** Not all solitaires are princess-specific. Blue Nile labels princess-compatible settings clearly — look for "Available with princess cut" in the filter.

**5. Place both in cart. Check total. Then buy.**

This sequence takes 20 minutes. It prevents budget overruns, setting mismatches, and the V-prong mistake.

