## How to Insure a Princess Cut Ring

Princess corners are the highest chip-risk point of any diamond shape. Insurance is not optional for a princess cut ring — it's the only backup when a corner chips.

**What you need:** A jewelry floater policy (also called a scheduled personal property endorsement), not a standard homeowner's policy. Homeowner's policies typically exclude jewelry or have a low cap ($1,000–$2,000 maximum). A floater covers the full replacement value with no deductible.

**Cost:** $1–$2 per $100 of insured value per year. A $4,000 ring runs $40–$80 per year.

**What to get first:** An independent appraisal from a GIA-certified appraiser (not the retailer). The appraisal should include the ring's replacement value, stone grade, and setting description. Take this to your insurance provider.

**When corner chips happen:** If a corner chips with a jewelry floater in place, the repair ($400–$800) or full replacement is covered. Without insurance, that's out-of-pocket. Blue Nile's return policy covers defects, not damage — a chip from daily wear is not a defect.

Get the appraisal and insurance within 30 days of receiving the ring. Don't wait.

---

## Princess Cut vs. Round: Which Should You Choose?

The most common comparison before committing to a princess cut.

**Shape and silhouette:** Princess reads square from above. Round reads circular. Princess is contemporary and architectural. Round is classic and universally recognized as an engagement ring shape.

**Setting requirements:** Round fits any solitaire with no special prong requirements. Princess requires V-prongs. That narrows settings slightly, but the V-prong options available for princess cuts are well-made and widely available at Blue Nile.

**Price difference:** Princess cut diamonds run 10–20% less than round brilliants of equivalent grade and weight. The round brilliant's Tolkowsky ideal cut commands a premium. A GIA 1ct G-VS2 round runs $2,600–$3,000; the same grade princess runs $2,200–$2,500. The saving is real.

**Durability:** Round is the more durable shape — no exposed corners, no chip risk. Princess with V-prongs set correctly is a daily-wear ring. Without V-prongs, corner chips accumulate over years.

**Sparkle:** Round brilliant returns white light and fire across all viewing angles. Princess returns more directional fire — the chevron facets produce strong diagonal flashes at specific angles. Some buyers prefer the dramatic, angular sparkle of princess to the diffuse brilliance of round.

If you want a square ring with a contemporary look and you're willing to confirm V-prongs: princess is the right choice. If you want the safest shape with no corner risk and universal setting compatibility: round is the right choice.

For buyers who want the angular, square silhouette: the 10–20% stone saving and the distinctive sparkle make princess the better pick.

